WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.550
An invented Christchurch builder needs
to update a homeowner about their quote.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.310
The first prompt says: write
something about a quote.

3
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.550
That gives Claude very
little to work with,

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.470
so the draft can fill
the gaps with guesses.

5
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.750
A start date. A price.

6
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.430
Neither came from you.

7
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.230
A useful prompt carries the facts,

8
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.430
and describes the finished draft.

9
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.870
Write your source notes
first, then the instruction.

10
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.390
Keep them separate, so you
can see which parts are facts.

11
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.630
Claude's own help pages say the same:

12
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.470
be specific, avoid vague requests,

13
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.830
and include relevant context.

14
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.070
Start with the task: draft
an email to the homeowner.

15
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.270
Add the context: the
site visit is complete,

16
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.590
and the quote is still under review.

17
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.150
Give a role: an office administrator,

18
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.470
drafting for the manager to review.

19
00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.470
A role guides the writing.

20
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.510
It doesn't give Claude
professional qualifications.

21
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.190
Then describe the output: a subject line,

22
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.190
and a body under a hundred words,

23
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.110
in New Zealand English,
with a friendly tone.

24
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.270
Name the audience as well: a homeowner
who isn't in the building trade,

25
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.830
so plain words beat jargon.

26
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.830
Now the boundary. Do not invent a price,

27
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.190
a due date, or a guarantee.

28
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.990
That one line protects the business.

29
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.630
Examples help too. One invented
sentence can show your office's tone.

30
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.750
For a table, give one made-up example row,

31
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.750
so Claude copies the structure.

32
00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.190
Don't use real customer
emails as examples.

33
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.190
Then read the draft against the facts,

34
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.030
sentence by sentence.

35
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.350
Underline every claim you could check,

36
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.910
and compare each one with your notes.

37
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.750
Say the draft promises: your
quote will arrive on Friday.

38
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.070
Friday wasn't in your notes.

39
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.790
If a claim has no source, remove it,

40
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.150
or mark it as a question
for the person responsible.

41
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.790
When you ask for a change, be specific.

42
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.630
Not, make it better.

43
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.750
Instead: shorten the body to sixty words,

44
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.790
keep the facts, and
remove the added deadline.

45
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.230
Changes to style can also change meaning,

46
00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.790
so check again after every revision.

47
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.150
A tidy answer still needs a human review.

48
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.550
When a prompt works, keep the pattern.

49
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.790
Leave empty spaces for the task,

50
00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.590
audience, source and limits, and fill
them with approved facts next time.

51
00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.270
Never save private information
in your prompt library.

52
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.670
A saved prompt is a starting
point for judgement,

53
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.990
not permission to skip the check.

54
00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.270
Try the before-and-after
exercise in the next lesson.
